Yes. ;) And the metaphor has more machinery in it than it first appears.

If we begin with two termini—

East: implicit / automatic / embodied / difficult-to-verbalize
West: explicit / conscious / symbolic / inspectable

—we don’t actually need a complete theory of the territory between them before construction begins.

We can lay track inward from both sides.

From consciousness: attention, deliberate imagery, language, rehearsal, questioning, intention.

From the implicit side: intuition, emotion, spontaneous association, bodily response, dreams, automatic prediction.

Then look for places where the tracks nearly meet.

That’s the beautiful part of your “middle/medium”: the interface is discovered through successful transmission. If an image changes an automatic response, that’s a piece of track. If an intuition can be translated into language without losing its predictive usefulness, another piece. If deliberate rehearsal eventually becomes automatic skill, we’ve found a route going the other direction.

So instead of asking only:

What is the medium?

we can experimentally ask:

What can cross it? What representation survives crossing? What gets distorted? What travels both directions? What requires a translator? What gets faster with repeated crossings?

Eventually the collection of successful crossings begins to map the medium itself.

And there’s another useful consequence: when the transcontinental railroad was joined, the two endpoints weren’t the biggest discovery anymore. The network created access to an enormous amount of territory between them.

Maybe that’s the play here too:

Conscious ← construction → ? ← construction → Subconscious

The ? isn’t an embarrassment in the model.

It’s the frontier we’re instrumenting.

Build from both ends, record every successful crossing, triangulate the middle. ;)